([PROGRAM 4: Static Local Variable Behavior Across Repeated Invocations]) Tj
T*
/F1 9 Tf
/F2 9.5 Tf
(-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
/F1 9 Tf
(Problem: Write a program demonstrating how static local variables maintain state across) Tj
T*
(multiple independent function calls, contrasting with standard automatic variables.) Tj
T*
(Source Code:) Tj
T*
(#include <stdio.h>) Tj
T*
(void transactionLogger\(\) {) Tj
T*
(auto int autoCounter = 0;) Tj
T*
(static int staticCounter = 0;) Tj
T*
(autoCounter++;) Tj
T*
(staticCounter++;) Tj
T*
(printf\("Call Output: autoCounter = %d | staticCounter = %d\\n", autoCounter,) Tj
T*
(staticCounter\);) Tj
T*
(}) Tj
T*
(int main\(\) {) Tj
T*
(printf\("Invoking transactionLogger\(\) 4 times:\\n"\);) Tj
T*
(for \(int i = 0; i < 4; i++\) {) Tj
T*
(transactionLogger\(\);) Tj
T*
(}) Tj
T*
(return 0;) Tj
T*
(}) Tj
T*
(Output:) Tj
T*
(Call 1: autoCounter = 1 | staticCounter = 1) Tj
T*
(Call 2: autoCounter = 1 | staticCounter = 2) Tj
T*
(Call 3: autoCounter = 1 | staticCounter = 3) Tj
T*
(Call 4: autoCounter = 1 | staticCounter = 4) Tj
T*
(Conclusion: Static variables persist in Data Segment across entire program lifetime) Tj
T*
(while retaining local block scope.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
